Perhaps I should deal at once with the question of inconsistency. I remember that when the point was raised for the first time I made the statement referred to a moment ago by the hon. member, namely that relief scales were not controlled by the dominion government. That statement was literally correct. We do not, for instance, set a scale in Alberta, Saskatchewan, Manitoba or any other province. But at the same time we did, under the agreement to which the hon. member refers, covering the last quarter of the last fiscal year, set a maximum which would prevent relief payments from coming into competition with normal earnings of relief recipients.
It is quite possible I may not have made that as clear as I ought to have done when the matter was first brought up; but it did come up on a later occasion, and I believe I cleared it up then. That was during the
debate on the unemployment and agricultural assistance measure. Actually both that recommendation, and the one with regard to budgeting, came from the national employment commission, and it was only adopted after conferences between the officials of the dominion Department of Labour and those of the various provincial departments of labour. I will not say that all the provinces were wholly in agreement with those two proposals; but certainly it was the consensus of opinion that the adoption of that maximum, which would prevent relief from being exploited, as it were, as against work, would be a satisfactory procedure. The one as to budgeting was really designed, in a large measure, in the interests of the relief recipient, but was also designed to prevent one who received casual work from going immediately upon the relief rolls, when that casual work ended. The hon. member may say that this is imposing a hardship upon those men. It was not designed to do that. It was, however, intended to prevent what might readily have become a rather serious abuse. I shall read the recommendation from the report of the national employment commission.
-grants in aid to be made under conditions which will include the following:
(e) Observance of the principle of maintenance of incentive to accept employment^ by relating the maximum aid to actual earnings of unskilled labour in each centre or regional division, while preserving standards of health and decency;
(d)	Limitation of shelter allowances to a definite relationship with assessments and total shelter disbursements to a reasonable proportion of total cost of aid granted;
The next requirement is:
(e)	Requirement of "Cancellation of Aid" cards to provide running records of earnings permitting of prompt assessment of further need for aid, this in order to facilitate the acceptance of casual employment;
In other words, it was designed not only to help the relief recipient in his budgeting, when he went into employment, but also to make it more readily possible for him to go back on relief, if he were entitled to do so. In other words, there would be there a definite and accurate record as to what he had received while employed.
